# tetron-veilid host inventory.
#
# Copy this file to hosts.conf (gitignored -- your real infra is your own
# business) and edit. One host per line:
#
#   <name>  <target>
#
# <name> is a label used only within this tool (log lines, --host
# arguments) -- it does not need to match the real hostname.
#
# <target> is either the literal word "local" (run commands directly on
# this machine) or an SSH target (user@host, anything `ssh` itself accepts,
# including a Host alias from your own ~/.ssh/config).
#
# Unlike tetron-relay (one shared server for a whole fleet), each entry
# here is normally the SAME machine a tetron node already runs on -- you
# run `tetron-veilid up` once per node that wants --veilid connectivity,
# using "local" if this controller is that node, or an SSH target if it
# is a different one. The SSH user needs passwordless sudo (or run as
# root directly) since bringup writes to /usr/local/bin, /etc/tetron-veilid,
# and installs a systemd unit.
#
# Lines starting with # are comments. Blank lines are ignored.

#local  local
#node2  ssh:user@node2.example.com
